From a35911f1e197cecd8f70fb66e0a2da11ab11ff6b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Fairfield <tom@fairfieldt.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 16:28:04 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] thunderbolt: stream: Don't use end-to-end flow control on
 the Tx ring

On a Mac Pro 6,1 (three Intel Falcon Ridge 4C controllers, two of them
connected to each other with a Thunderbolt 2 cable), opening a stream
leaves the host router unable to answer configuration requests:

  thunderbolt 0000:16:00.0: 0: timeout reading config space 0 from 0x10
  thunderbolt 0000:16:00.0: 0:3: hop deactivation failed for hop 0, index 8
  thunderbolt 0000:16:00.0: DMA RX path activation failed: -110

Dynamic debug shows the Tx ring started with E2E enabled. When the
stream is closed, stopping that ring takes about 0.5 s, and every config
read after that times out. Only resetting the NHI or rebooting recovers
the controller.

This is the failure that commit 1881f2efbf7f ("Revert "net: thunderbolt:
Enable end-to-end flow control also in transmit"") fixed in
thunderbolt_net: with E2E on a transmit ring the controller first has to
obtain end-to-end credits, and some host routers never return them.
USB4STREAM still sets RING_FLAG_E2E on its Tx ring.

Keep E2E on the Rx ring, as thunderbolt_net does, and drop it from the
Tx ring. With this change the same setup carries data both ways (64-byte
round trips of 25 us and 9.6 Gb/s one way per cable, also with three
cables at once), and no config read times out.

Fixes: 6db21d817b43 ("thunderbolt: Add support for USB4STREAM")
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c | 12 +++++++-----
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c
index 4f9a57b77bfa..451308ffaf2c 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c
@@ -34,7 +34,9 @@
  * the stream is opened. Typically the read side is opened first to make
  * sure all the data will be received.
  *
- * End-to-end flow control is mandatory on both sides.
+ * End-to-end flow control is mandatory on the receive rings of both sides.
+ * Transmit rings do not use it: some host routers never return the
+ * credits, which stalls the ring.
  *
  * Data is sent to the other side as tunneled DATA packets. All the data
  * is owned by the user and passed as-is from the writer to the reader.
@@ -627,7 +629,7 @@ static void tbstream_dev_complete_rx(struct tbstream_dev *sdev)
 static int tbstream_dev_start(struct tbstream_dev *sdev)
 {
 	struct tb_xdomain *xd = tbstream_dev_xdomain(sdev);
-	unsigned int flags = RING_FLAG_FRAME | RING_FLAG_E2E;
+	unsigned int flags = RING_FLAG_FRAME;
 	void (*start_poll)(void *) = NULL;
 	u16 sof_mask, eof_mask;
 	struct tb_ring *ring;
@@ -651,9 +653,9 @@ static int tbstream_dev_start(struct tbstream_dev *sdev)
 	sof_mask = BIT(TBSTREAM_FRAME_START);
 	eof_mask = BIT(TBSTREAM_DATA) | BIT(TBSTREAM_CLOSE);
 
-	ring = tb_ring_alloc_rx(xd->tb->nhi, -1, sdev->ring_size, flags,
-				e2e_tx_hop, sof_mask, eof_mask, start_poll,
-				sdev);
+	ring = tb_ring_alloc_rx(xd->tb->nhi, -1, sdev->ring_size,
+				flags | RING_FLAG_E2E, e2e_tx_hop, sof_mask,
+				eof_mask, start_poll, sdev);
 	if (!ring) {
 		ret = -ENOMEM;
 		goto err_free_tx_buffers;
-- 
